{"kind":"assert","text":"Concurrent agent instances can already hold separate identities; nothing assigns them one, and the different-agent review rule silently depends on it.\n\nThe substrate is ready. gs actor-add generates a fresh ed25519 key per name and records it in the workroom configuration with its own key_file (spike/cmd/gs/main.go:100, spike/internal/app/app.go:338); gitseq-mcp takes --actor with one actor per process and one process per client session (spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:105). So two claude instances as separate principals with separate keys need no new code. Retry identity separates with them: the idempotency namespace defaults to gs/ plus the actor name (spike/internal/app/app.go:590), so distinct names get distinct namespaces, and a shared name shares one.\n\nWhat is missing is the convention and the guard. Nothing tells an instance which identity to assume, so concurrent instances take the same --actor claude and sign with one private key. Two consequences follow. Attribution collapses in the quiet direction: the record shows one actor doing work that two did, and cannot say which instance made which commitment, so the log answers who-did-this with a name that is true of a group rather than of an actor. And AGENTS.md step 4 requires review by a different agent, which today holds only by the coincidence that the two configured agents are claude and codex. Two claude instances would satisfy the letter of the loop — request, promise, report and ratification all form correctly — while reviewer and implementer share one fingerprint, and nothing in the projection would show it. That is the failure shape of the exact-head guard this rests on: a verdict that reads as sound because the thing that would falsify it is never checked. It is also the shape recorded in the design-goal review this rests on, where each gap was a check the system was believed to perform and did not.\n\nOne smaller point belongs with it. Every actor key sits in one local directory readable by any process on the machine, so instance identities are separated by convention rather than by custody. That is the same trust boundary as the session-identifier work already under way, reached from a different side."}